1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:03,200
Far from Earth, a health reading changes.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:08,030
Is it a warning, a passing fluctuation, or a
sensor issue?

3
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:12,830
Our challenge: help astronauts understand their
health on long missions,

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:15,020
when support may be delayed.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,960
We built PROHOR: a research prototype that turns
scattered readings into context,

6
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:27,100
compares them with personal history, and helps a
human decide what to review next.

7
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:31,460
We are team Skorpe Monad.

8
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:35,420
Tufayel Ahmed, our team lead, main developer and
designer.

9
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:39,620
Annapurna Das Protyasha, researcher and concept
maker.

10
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:42,388
Rakthim Deb Rudro, advisor.

11
00:00:42,388 --> 00:00:45,210
Mir Shahi Ahmod, data scientist.

12
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:48,540
And Tonusri Deb Diggi, video and photo editor.

13
00:00:52,073 --> 00:00:53,623
The idea is simple.

14
00:00:53,783 --> 00:00:55,603
First, gather readings.

15
00:00:55,613 --> 00:01:00,853
Next, compare each reading with prior days,
using a transparent personal baseline.

16
00:01:00,863 --> 00:01:04,293
Then prepare a routine follow-up and a handover.

17
00:01:04,293 --> 00:01:07,663
PROHOR gives context; it does not diagnose
illness.

18
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:10,090
Here is one simulated example.

19
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:15,295
Today’s sleep is 5.6 hours; the person’s
previous median is 7.4.

20
00:01:15,295 --> 00:01:20,350
PROHOR uses the median and interquartile range
of prior days to flag a change.

21
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:24,800
Its band is an unvalidated prototype rule, not a
medical threshold.

22
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:28,450
The health atlas makes the information easier to
explore.

23
00:01:29,210 --> 00:01:33,130
Choose a body system to see its available
indicators and context.

24
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:35,625
A color points to a reading that needs review,

25
00:01:35,625 --> 00:01:36,940
or limited data.

26
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:38,740
It does not diagnose an organ.

27
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:43,040
PROHOR uses NASA’s Open Science Data Repository,

28
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:46,970
OSD-575, in its research explorer.

29
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:51,950
The Inspiration4 blood serum metabolic panel
supplies real measurements behind these

30
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:56,000
charts, with original values, units, and source
rows.

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,880
This is historical research, separate from our
simulated crew,

32
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,490
and does not validate its sleep flags.

33
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:07,777
NOAA’s planetary Kp adds Earth geomagnetic
context.

34
00:02:08,087 --> 00:02:09,727
Cabin readings are separate.

35
00:02:09,767 --> 00:02:11,847
Neither proves the cause of a health change,

36
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:14,617
or represents a crew member’s personal radiation
dose.

37
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:17,780
Here is a real import in the prototype.

38
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:21,800
A sample CSV brings readings into the local
browser.

39
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:26,630
Required fields and units are checked, and an
invalid row rejects the import.

40
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:30,800
Local inputs remain separate from NASA research
and the simulated mission.

41
00:02:31,370 --> 00:02:35,950
After the first successful load and cache, the
workspace can work offline.

42
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:37,950
We reloaded it without a network.

43
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:41,470
Records stay in the browser, with no hidden
synchronization.

44
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:45,160
This browser storage is not a secure clinical
record system.

45
00:02:46,043 --> 00:02:48,583
A flag is a starting point, not a decision.

46
00:02:48,653 --> 00:02:52,903
The care plan offers routine follow-up, such as
protecting a sleep window.

47
00:02:52,913 --> 00:02:57,183
A structured handover brings the reading and
crew notes together for review.

48
00:02:57,183 --> 00:03:01,483
It can be exported or printed; nothing is sent
automatically.

49
00:03:02,077 --> 00:03:03,297
Why does this matter?

50
00:03:03,307 --> 00:03:05,537
Our goal is not to replace doctors.

51
00:03:05,537 --> 00:03:09,002
It is to help a crew notice a change, understand
the evidence,

52
00:03:09,002 --> 00:03:11,492
and bring a clearer story to a human reviewer,

53
00:03:11,492 --> 00:03:13,917
even when a connection is unavailable.

54
00:03:13,957 --> 00:03:18,812
On longer missions, this transparent,
offline-capable workflow could make information

55
00:03:18,812 --> 00:03:21,747
easier to review when support is far away.

56
00:03:22,337 --> 00:03:24,577
A number alone is not a story.

57
00:03:24,587 --> 00:03:27,757
PROHOR brings the change, the person’s baseline,

58
00:03:27,797 --> 00:03:31,767
and the evidence together, so a crew can prepare
for human review,

59
00:03:31,767 --> 00:03:33,967
even when a connection is unavailable.

60
00:03:34,127 --> 00:03:39,307
Our next step is to test this research prototype
with clinicians and mission operators.

61
00:03:39,407 --> 00:03:41,287
It is not a medical device.

62
00:03:41,297 --> 00:03:43,587
We are Skorpe Monad.

63
00:03:43,587 --> 00:03:46,957
PROHOR: a quiet watch for a long journey.

